  "image_name": "0cd0404e20ab0c98e8136110d211ff3e.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how hydroxychloroquine may affect the macula by acting on retinal pigment epithelial cells next to photoreceptors. It begins with the eye and zooms into the macular retina, showing the layered arrangement of ganglion cells, photoreceptors, and the pigment epithelium that supports them.\n\nThe enlarged schematic focuses on the interface between photoreceptor outer segments and retinal pigment epithelial cells. Three main effects are highlighted. First, hydroxychloroquine appears to accumulate in lysosomes and raise their pH, which would interfere with normal lysosomal function. Second, this disruption is linked to buildup of lipofuscin within the pigment epithelium, shown as yellow granules, alongside melanin-containing structures. Third, the figure suggests impairment of the visual cycle, indicated by arrows between photoreceptors and the pigment epithelium, implying reduced recycling or processing needed for photoreceptor function.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a mechanism for macular toxicity: hydroxychloroquine concentrates in retinal pigment epithelial cells, alters intracellular degradation pathways, promotes waste accumulation, and disrupts the supportive exchange between these cells and photoreceptors. The emphasis is on how these combined changes could damage central retinal function.",
